  {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/brcomp#Cap",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Non-traveled edge formation of reinforced concrete or prestressed concrete bridges, which can also be formed as kerbs.\n\n caps have to fulfil various functions. In addition to protecting the load-bearing bridge structure, they are used for anchoring passive protective devices and as bicycle and/or pedestrian paths. Due to their exposed position, they are exposed to particularly strong attacks, e.g. frost-thaw salt attacks. In addition, there are dynamic loads caused by traffic on the bridges. Even if the bridge caps undergo optical changes as a result of wear, load or weathering during the planned service life, concrete bridge caps are safe and durable if constructive and technical rules are observed."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "de",
        "@value": "Kappe"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
